From cfe3598dc36181bef39ea8a560203235339de03f Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: daveey <daveey@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 13 Nov 2026 11:43:56 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] [daveey] kanban remote notices: publish one host copy of the
 remote notice index per replica state
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Design t_01a1d45d75851804 §2c (review item R8); card t_01a1d471735508b4.

Why: every backend's 5 s notice poller rebuilt the same remote index for the same replica state.
In a steady hub serve, `remote_index_projection.snapshot` was 11.5% of samples and
`kanban_remote_observations.observe` was 2.1%. Each was memoized only per process.

What:
- New `tui_gateway/kanban_remote_index.py` publishes `<ledger dir>/remote-index.db`:
  meta (key = replica file identity + max seq, me, ledger file identity, hub_db, built_at),
  `cards(owner, key, seq, state, task, obs, fresh, first_seen)` and
  `links(profile, session, owner, key, ord)`. Each publish is one transaction that writes only the
  rows whose seq/state/observation changed (per-seq delta; a full rewrite only when the replica
  file changes).
- `collect` → `_chat_index`: when the published key equals the replica's current key, it reads
  only its own `(profile, session ∈ lineage)` rows, plus first-seen times from those rows, so it
  skips both `_index` and `observe`. On a stale key, a missing/corrupt db or a different ledger, it
  computes locally exactly as before and offers the result for publishing.
- Publishing never waits. The build lock is a non-blocking flock, sqlite timeout is 0, a busy
  publish is skipped, and nothing touches the index db while `kanban_remote_notices._lock` is held.
- Holder: a host loop lease `kanban-remote-index` (gateway > serve) runs a change-driven refresher.
  It does one `PRAGMA data_version` fingerprint per second and rebuilds only when the replica
  changes, so readers normally hit. The holder is started from `collect` (serve) and from the
  gateway's remote owner poll. The holder is optional: with it dead, the first reader after a
  change computes locally and publishes.
- Readers reuse parsed task payloads per (replica file, owner, key, seq). A chat with hundreds of
  linked cards parses only the cards that changed.

Bench (gate: a publish per replica change must cost ≤ today's incremental per-seq snapshot in one
process). Copy of a live replica (16,410 task rows), 20 one-card changes, median ms:

                              me=macbook (534 chats, 1,684 cards)   me=hub (436 chats, 1,465 cards)
  today, per process per change:
    snapshot (incremental)    40.8                                  32.9
    observe                   55.9                                  43.1
  published:
    full publish              117.8  (> snapshot: rejected)          83.3
    delta publish (shipped)   36.8   (≤ snapshot)                    22.2
    reader: key + hit         1.6 + 5.0 (biggest chat, 524 cards)   1.5 + 3.0
    rows written per change   1                                     1

A full publish per change fails the gate (118 > 41 ms), so the module publishes per-seq deltas,
which pass (37 ≤ 41 ms). Each additional backend's per-change cost drops from snapshot + observe
(~97 ms) to ~7 ms.

Tests (tests/tui_gateway/test_kanban_remote_index.py, 11): reader hit returns the same notices as
local compute (with `_index` and `observe` forbidden); stale key → local compute + publish;
missing / corrupt / other-ledger db → local compute and repair; no index I/O or wait under
`_lock` while another process holds the build lock and a write transaction; a notice reaches the
chat within the 5 s poll with the holder dead; the holder republishes on change only while leased
and never when unchanged; a delta writes only the changed row; only changed payloads are re-parsed;
a replica without seq never publishes.
RED: on the base, the file fails at import (module absent). GREEN: 11/11. Mutation check: 8 of 8
mutants killed (no hits, ignore key, publish under _lock, standby publishes, full rewrite, no corrupt
recovery, blocking build lock, re-parse all).
Neighbours: 54 files / 749 tests importing the touched modules pass. `scripts/check --base
origin/main`: 19 checks ok.
